[{:db/id "q-128332/field-1/143cd9672d07ddf40c1923dbe6e7432f53eb7aadadb0aadd0ee846fd5e813843" :answer/id #uuid "f66003b7-cfcc-5de3-93c9-63ff780a4390" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128332/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8be7543-a7cf-5018-8b39-c018360a6399" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128332/field-1/143cd9672d07ddf40c1923dbe6e7432f53eb7aadadb0aadd0ee846fd5e813843" :answer-field/choices ["q-128332/field-1/143cd9672d07ddf40c1923dbe6e7432f53eb7aadadb0aadd0ee846fd5e813843"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128332" :question/id #uuid "2bfbd25a-1326-5d7c-9e85-767cc1198974" :question/math-academy-id "q-128332" :question/problem "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(2).$ In part 1, we found\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{7(5-4x)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{7(5-4\\cdot 2)}{(2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot (-3)}{(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is $-\\frac{21}{25}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-128332/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ffbc4bb-e179-543f-bcb5-3aaf6540a8a1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128332"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128339/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "d01d4f0d-5a05-513a-9e4f-ec33484a4d10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128339/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "83fdb9a8-8f9d-5b98-bf33-701f4df683f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128339/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-128339/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128339/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "ffc904a0-3682-593e-8517-2d97321b88a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128339/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "3f601664-4371-5ee4-b0c8-52912a576684" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128339/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-128339/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128339" :question/id #uuid "5f5dc83b-4d6f-5a90-9b27-05db05e5bf2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-128339" :question/problem "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{4}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nDetermine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}=\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ 7 & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot 3+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 7 & =7A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A=1$ and $B=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-128339/field-1" "q-128339/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128339"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128333/field-1/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer/id #uuid "fc2bc84d-e21c-5d61-b478-270c07edb55d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128333/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "91e0990c-db75-5693-9748-4087db6ca9ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128333/field-1/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer-field/choices ["q-128333/field-1/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128333" :question/id #uuid "33f175dd-a9d4-5d3d-aba6-42b4e89e487b" :question/math-academy-id "q-128333" :question/problem "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 1, we obtained\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{7(5-4x)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7(5-4x)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 7(5-4x) & =0 \\\\ 5-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\n* Second, we find the points in the domain of $f(x)$ where ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. Factoring our expression for ${f}^{′}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{7(5-4x)}{[(2x+1)(x-3)]^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=3.$ But these points do not lie in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},3).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the only critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-128333/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128333"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128340/field-1/e9eb6f03bad2f79992065aa424d0dcf6c52f63d542b0d33351010ccc7ec488e4" :answer/id #uuid "61ee586d-0612-5c3e-a75f-270583bfa339" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\ln(9/2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128340/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "74826e0f-e86b-5ce8-b382-97a7cd0aa465" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128340/field-1/e9eb6f03bad2f79992065aa424d0dcf6c52f63d542b0d33351010ccc7ec488e4" :answer-field/choices ["q-128340/field-1/e9eb6f03bad2f79992065aa424d0dcf6c52f63d542b0d33351010ccc7ec488e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128340" :question/id #uuid "0e117787-42d5-505b-9374-c2505266a725" :question/math-academy-id "q-128340" :question/problem "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{4}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nDetermine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima.\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the indefinite integral first. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the improper integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{a-3}{2a+1}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{9}|) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1-\\frac{3}{a}}{2+\\frac{1}{a}}|)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{9}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-128340/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128340"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128335/field-1/b6c118b614c1b0209bad4527f236eb0aaea69e3ffb11cc59bc7f8d0d8941a045" :answer/id #uuid "0dde75d3-5a2b-56c2-aaa5-3148a3d65dc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128335/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0951cc94-6b35-5134-8494-4538786fc2e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128335/field-1/b6c118b614c1b0209bad4527f236eb0aaea69e3ffb11cc59bc7f8d0d8941a045" :answer-field/choices ["q-128335/field-1/b6c118b614c1b0209bad4527f236eb0aaea69e3ffb11cc59bc7f8d0d8941a045"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128335" :question/id #uuid "0bb8566e-0896-530f-9a63-4498d25abe00" :question/math-academy-id "q-128335" :question/problem "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=7$ and $v(x)=2{x}^{2}-5x-3,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=0,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4x-5.\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x){v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot (2{x}^{2}-5x-3)-7\\cdot (4x-5)}{{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7\\cdot (4x-5)}{{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(5-4x)}{{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-128335/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128335"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128660/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "c08e93f0-7bbd-5d32-8fa8-5eb7e60ebeaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128660/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2287e71e-45d3-5ad8-8307-3c9107596ba9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128660/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-128660/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128660" :question/id #uuid "cf79f28e-194a-5f6c-9e11-0c8737ed5396" :question/math-academy-id "q-128660" :question/problem "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "In part 2 we found that ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{21}{25}.$ Also,\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\frac{7}{2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3}=-\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5-25f(x)$ and $v=g(x)+3.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-25{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot (g(x)+3)-(5-25f(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x)+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot (g(2)+3)-(5-25f(2))\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2)+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25\\cdot (-\\frac{21}{25})\\cdot (-2+3)-(5-25\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{(-2+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =21-20 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-128660/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128660"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128336/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "93aaceda-b6ab-531e-8d49-1d20aa189f6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128336/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4033eb5f-f2d3-516f-b7a9-a05c641158a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128336/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-128336/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128336/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "f5ddb6cd-9e1b-5f1e-a24a-c2c1f0e45f35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128336/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "852e1644-4343-5c83-9c3f-d540af58cfd7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-128336/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-128336/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128336" :question/id #uuid "4346e073-2d30-5ade-a6c2-25bdeeed7387" :question/math-academy-id "q-128336" :question/problem "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{4}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nDetermine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima." :question/worked-solution "We have the derivative \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{7(4x-5)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the critical point $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$ We use the first derivative test to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{7(4\\cdot 0-5)}{(2\\cdot {0}^{2}-5\\cdot 0-3)^{2}}=\\frac{35}{9}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{7(4\\cdot 2-5)}{(2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3)^{2}}=-\\frac{21}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4})$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $(\\frac{5}{4},3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\n \nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n* At $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5}{4}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ has $1$ local maximum and $0$ local minima on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-128336/field-1" "q-128336/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128336"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
